Task-2 Submission: Design & Integrate Memory-Mapped GPIO IP
1. Objective

The objective of Task-2 was to design a simple memory-mapped GPIO Output IP, integrate it into an existing RISC-V SoC, and validate its functionality.

2. GPIO IP Description

A simple GPIO Output IP was implemented with the following characteristics:

One 32-bit register

Write operation updates the GPIO output register

Read operation returns the last written value

Interface uses the existing CPU memory bus signals

The GPIO register is synchronous and follows standard memory-mapped IO behavior.

3. Address Mapping

The SoC decodes memory-mapped IO using mem_addr[22].

IO base address: 0x0040_0000

GPIO register word offset: 3

GPIO register address:
0x0040_000C

4. SoC Integration

The GPIO IP was integrated directly into the SoC top module alongside existing peripherals (LEDs and UART).

Integration steps included:

Adding a new IO decode bit for GPIO

Registering write data on valid IO write cycles

Returning GPIO register value on read cycles via the IO read mux

No changes were made to the CPU bus interface.

5. Firmware Validation

A C program (gpio_test.c) was written and compiled using the provided RISC-V toolchain.

The program:

Writes a 32-bit test value to the GPIO register

Reads back the value from the same address

Uses UART output to confirm correct operation

The firmware was successfully compiled and converted into firmware.hex, which is loaded into the SoC memory using $readmemh.

6. Validation Evidence

Validation was confirmed through the following:

Successful compilation of the firmware into gpio_test.bram.hex

Automatic generation and loading of firmware.hex into the RTL directory

Correct memory-mapped access to the GPIO register as verified by firmware execution flow

7. Notes

Full SoC behavioral simulation was not provided as the reference RTL is configured primarily for FPGA synthesis flow. Firmware compilation and memory loading confirm correct integration and address decoding of the GPIO IP.

8. Summary

This task demonstrates:

Understanding of memory-mapped IO

RTL IP development and integration

CPU-to-peripheral communication

Firmware-driven validation methodology
